# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# THIS ONE IS A PIN, NOT A CORRECTION, AND THAT IS THE REASON TO WRITE IT.
# Measured 2026-09-17 against this repository: branch protection already
# required exactly the five names below, and ci.yml on main (commit b9a9d82,
# run 35176029936) produced exactly those five and nothing else -- on main's
# check-runs and on the last pull_request check-suite (95263140349, #115)
# alike. Nothing is being fixed here. What is being fixed is that the list
# lived only on the settings page, where it has no diff, no reviewer and no
# history: the next matrix leg added to ci.yml reports under a new name,
# nobody adds it to the required set, and the repository is one job greener
# than it is tested with no commit to point at. The sibling rust-img-qcow2
# is that failure already -- it produces `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu`,
# which was never required, so a red leg there never blocked a merge. This
# file is where that divergence becomes a pull request instead of a surprise.
# See the constellation audit, diskjockey#197 (sections 1 and 7).
#
# Every name here runs on `pull_request`, which is the only kind that can
# gate one. release.yml's `test` and `publish to crates.io` jobs trigger on
# a `v*.*.*` tag, after the merge they would be gating has already happened,
# so declaring either would require a check that never reports on a pull
# request and would block every merge permanently. They are deliberately
# absent. No third-party reviewer app reports on this repository today;
# github-protect-main.sh filters `select(.app.slug=="github-actions")` on
# every one of its queries in any case, so one appearing later cannot become
# required by accident.
#
# There is no aggregate `ci-ok`-style job here that `needs:` the rest, so the
# real job names are declared. An aggregate is the better end state -- it
# makes the required set one name that cannot drift -- but adding one is a
# workflow change with its own reasoning and does not belong in the commit
# that writes down what is already true.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports, including the
# "test / <os>" job-matrix spelling, which has spaces around the slash
# because Actions renders `name: test / ${{ matrix.os }}`. `test (release)`
# has no spaces around its brackets and is not a matrix leg at all -- it is
# the job named `test-release` carrying `name: test (release)`. The two
# spellings are not interchangeable and a wrong one is a check that never
# reports.
#
# ADDED 2026-09-18 for #119: `oracle (external tools)`. It is the job
# that runs tests/oracle_tools.rs -- this crate's tables against sgdisk,
# sfdisk, blkid and partx, in both directions -- and it is the only
# check here that is not this crate marking its own homework. It reports
# under that exact name because ci.yml's job `oracle` carries
# `name: oracle (external tools)`; the spelling has one space either
# side of nothing and brackets with no spaces inside them, like
# `test (release)` above. Undeclared, it would be a job that can go red
# without blocking a merge, which is the failure the rest of this file
# was written about.
[checks]
	required = fmt
	required = oracle (external tools)
	required = test (release)
	required = test / macos-latest
	required = test / ubuntu-latest
	required = test / windows-latest
